not necessarily enough to avoid being hurt, but yes, being airborn would 
reduce the damage (however, this reduction of damage generally doesn't 
alter the outcome of being hit by a 2 ton object traveling at 50 miles 
per hour). For every action there is an equal and opposite reaction. You 
get hit by a boulder while floating in midair. In response to this, your 
body takes some damage, and you are thrown in the direction the boulder 
was moving. In response to this action, the air exerts a certain amount 
of force back upon you (friction). Naturally, the amount of force 
exerted upon you by air is less than would be exerted upon you had you 
been standing against a wall when you were hit by the boulder. So if the 
initial contact with the boulder doesn't kill you, the friction from the 
air certainly will not, and you will be shunted out of the way by the 
rounded nature of the boulder. The heaviness and speed of the boulder 
(which are both factors which have never been established, as far as I 
know) will determine how much damage you take. Some might make the 
argument that the boulders aren't round enough to shunt you out of the 
way. To preclude that, I would say that if they aren't that round, they 
probably will not be moving quickly enough to kill you, as their 
non-rounded nature would cause them to jump and hit the ceiling (which 
is apparently low enough to prevent you from levitating over the 
boulder) and the ensuing collision would slow the boulder quite a bit.


